            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Server-sent events let a server push text to the browser over one long-lived HTTP response.\n\n1. The browser opens the connection with a normal request and the header `Accept: text/event-stream`.\n2. The server answers with `Content-Type: text/event-stream` and keeps the response open instead of closing it.\n3. Each message is a few lines of text such as `data: {\"text\": \"Hello\"}`, ended by a blank line.\n4. The browser parses each message as it arrives, so the page can render a token at a time.\n5. If the connection drops, the page keeps what it already has and can ask the server to continue.\n\nThat last point is why a stream that stops halfway is an interruption, not an error."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
